object CodePointRepository {
    // Stored as chunked string literals rather than one intArrayOf(...) of
    // every character's code point -- see buildKotlinSource in lawgpt.html
    // for why.
    private val textChunks: List<String> = listOf(
        "[0m[1m1. Rules[0m\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 200 — Nature and goal[0m\n[0m[1mof contract interpretation[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative citing Restatement (Second) of\nContracts § 200 and Burton, Elements of Contract Interpretation\n§ 1.1\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Contract interpretation is the process of\ndetermining the meaning of the language of a contract, and its\ngoal is to ascertain the intent of the parties\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What is a court doing when it interprets a\ncontract, and what is it trying to find?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: A dispute over the meaning of contractual\nlanguage (in practice almost always a written contract)\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is the starting point for every\ninterpretation dispute; it frames the court's task as finding\nwhat the parties intended, using the methods set out below\n(textualism or contextualism, then a standard of interpretation)\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Party intent is often unclear and disputed, and\nlanguage is imperfect; the casebook lists five reasons contracts\nend up with gaps or ambiguities: (1) language is imperfect and\nevery reader brings a different background (R2d § 201 cmt b),\n(2) parties can't foresee every contingency, (3) the stakes\ndon't justify drafting for every known issue, (4) parties trust\neach other to be reasonable if problems arise, and (5) lawyers\ndraft poorly\n\n[0m[1mRule: Interpretation vs. construction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative citing Farnsworth, Contracts § 7.7\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Some courts separate interpretation (finding the\nmeaning the parties gave the language) from construction (the\ncourt's role in deciding the legal effect of that language), but\nthe casebook uses \"interpretation\" to cover both because modern\ntheorists find the distinction cumbersome and usually\nunnecessary\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Is there a separate doctrinal step for\ndeciding a contract's legal effect, apart from deciding what it\nmeans?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: None; this is a terminology convention\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: When a court or case uses \"construction,\" read it\nas part of the broader interpretive process unless the court\nclearly draws the distinction\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Some courts and writers still keep the two terms\nseparate\n\n[0m[1mRule: Textualism (plain meaning rule / four corners rule) —[0m\n[0m[1mStage One: the ambiguity determination[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative citing Farnsworth § 7.12,\nSilverstein (24 Chapman L. Rev. 89), Pioneer Peat, Inc. v.\nQuality Grassing & Servs., Inc. (Minn. Ct. App. 2002), Ashwood\nCap., Inc. v. OTG Mgmt., Inc. (N.Y. App. Div. 2012), Burton §\n4.2.3, and Chart 5.1\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Under textualism the judge first decides, as a\nquestion of law and looking only within the \"four corners\" of\nthe contract, whether the disputed language is ambiguous,\nmeaning \"reasonably susceptible\" to more than one meaning;\nextrinsic evidence may not be considered at this stage\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: In a textualist jurisdiction, how does a court\ndecide whether contract language is ambiguous, and what material\nmay it consider?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A disputed contract term\n    2. The judge (not the jury) decides ambiguity as a question\n    of law\n    3. The judge looks only at the contract itself; dictionaries\n    are allowed because they are not \"evidence\"\n    4. The language is ambiguous only if it is reasonably\n    susceptible to more than one meaning\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This rule is triggered whenever the parties\ndispute what contract language means in a textualist court; if\nthe judge finds the language unambiguous, the case can be\ndecided early (motion to dismiss, summary judgment, or another\npreliminary proceeding); if the judge finds it ambiguous, the\ncase moves to Stage Two\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The casebook warns that there are \"innumerable\ngradations\" of textualism and contextualism in the case law and\nthat the doctrine is \"extraordinarily convoluted,\" with\nirreconcilable cases in nearly every jurisdiction; most scholars\nbelieve textualism is the majority approach\n\n[0m[1mRule: Textualism — consequence of finding no ambiguity (apply[0m\n[0m[1mthe plain meaning)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative citing Burton § 4.2.3, Seaco Ins.\nCo. v. Barbosa (Mass. 2002), and Salewski v. Music (N.Y. App.\nDiv. 2017); also applied in Prochazka (citing Roberts\nContracting Co. and Fryer v. Boyett)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: If the court decides the contract is unambiguous,\nit applies the unambiguous \"plain meaning\" to the facts as a\nmatter of law, and the judge never reviews extrinsic evidence\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What happens once a textualist court decides\nthe language is unambiguous?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The judge has found at Stage One that the\nlanguage is not reasonably susceptible to more than one meaning\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This lets the case be resolved on a motion to\ndismiss, a motion for summary judgment, or another preliminary\nproceeding without a trial; it is what the Pope County Circuit\nCourt did in Prochazka\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated in the reading for unambiguous text,\nbut the finding of no ambiguity can be reversed on appeal, as\nhappened in Prochazka\n\n[0m[1mRule: Textualism — Stage Two: resolving the ambiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative citing Silverstein, Archer v. DDK\nHoldings LLC (Tex. App. 2015), Burton § 4.2.3, Nycal Corp. v.\nInoco PLC (S.D.N.Y. 1997), Restatement (Second) § 212(2) & cmt\ne, § 202 cmt b, and Chart 5.1\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Once the contract is found ambiguous, its meaning\nbecomes a question of fact; the factfinder may consider the text\nplus all relevant extrinsic evidence, judged from the parties'\nperspective when the contract was formed; the judge resolves the\nambiguity if the evidence is absent or so one-sided that there\nis no genuine issue of material fact, and otherwise the jury\nresolves it at trial if a reasonable jury could rule for either\nside\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Once a contract is found ambiguous, who\ndecides what it means, and on what evidence?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A Stage One finding that the contract is ambiguous\n    2. Consideration of the text and all relevant extrinsic\n    evidence (e.g., documents and testimony about preliminary\n    negotiations, testimony about industry practice)\n    3. Judge decides, typically on summary judgment, if no\n    extrinsic evidence is offered or the evidence overwhelmingly\n    favors one side\n    4. Jury decides if a reasonable jury could rule for either\n    side, unless a jury has been waived\n    5. Meaning is assessed as of the moment of contract\n    formation\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This explains why a finding of ambiguity usually\ndefeats summary judgment, as in Prochazka, where the court\nremanded so a trier of fact could decide how the parties\nintended the contract to operate\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: When the extrinsic evidence is one-sided, the\njudge can still resolve the ambiguity on summary judgment\n\n[0m[1mRule: Contextualism — Stage One uses extrinsic evidence[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative citing Burton §§ 4.1, 4.2.2, Winet\nv. Price (Cal. Ct. App. 1992), BNC Mortg., Inc. v. Tax Pros,\nInc. (Wash. Ct. App. 2002), Restatement (Second) §§ 202(1), 202\ncmt d, 212 cmt b, and Chart 5.2\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Contextualism uses the same two-stage process as\ntextualism, but at Stage One the judge must consider both the\ncontract language and the extrinsic evidence the parties offer\nin deciding whether the contract is \"reasonably susceptible\" to\nthe meanings each side advances\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: In a contextualist jurisdiction, what evidence\nmay the judge consider in deciding whether a contract is\nambiguous?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The judge decides ambiguity based on the text plus all\n    relevant extrinsic evidence (the whole context)\n    2. If the text and extrinsic evidence overwhelmingly support\n    one party, the judge rules for that party (summary judgment,\n    directed verdict, or a similar device)\n    3. If a reasonable jury could rule for either party, the\n    jury (or the judge as trier of fact in a nonjury case)\n    decides at Stage Two\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Contextualism lets a party show a \"latent\"\nambiguity, one that appears only once context is considered; the\nRestatement (Second) endorses this approach (\"Words and other\nconduct are interpreted in the light of all the circumstances,\"\n§ 202(1); \"Meaning is inevitably dependent on context,\" § 202\ncmt d)\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Even under contextualism, the contract language\nremains the most important evidence of meaning (§ 212 cmt b);\nthe casebook defers full treatment of contextualism to Section\nB, after the parol evidence rule\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Both camps generally let the factfinder consider\nall extrinsic evidence at Stage Two; the core disagreement is\nover what may be considered at Stage One\n\n[0m[1mRule: Patent vs. latent ambiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative (CB p. 392)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Under textualism, the ambiguity must appear on the\nface of the agreement (\"patent,\" \"intrinsic,\" or \"facial\")\nbefore extrinsic evidence may be considered; under\ncontextualism, extrinsic evidence may be used to establish an\nambiguity (\"latent\" or \"extrinsic\")\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What kinds of ambiguity does each interpretive\napproach recognize?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Patent ambiguity: visible from the contract text alone\n    2. Latent ambiguity: shown through extrinsic evidence of\n    context\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Put simply, textualism recognizes only patent\nambiguities, while contextualism recognizes both patent and\nlatent ambiguities\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The casebook warns this formulation is \"too\nsimple,\" and later material adds nuance\n\n[0m[1mRule: Arkansas interpretation principles applied in Prochazka[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding / statements of law in Prochazka v.\nBee-Three Development, LLC (Ark. Ct. App. 2015), citing Keller\nv. Safeco, Elam v. First Unum, Singletary v. Singletary, Zulpo\nv. Farm Bureau, and State Auto Prop. & Cas. Ins. Co. v. Ark.\nDep't of Envt'l Quality\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Circuit courts first decide whether a contract is\nambiguous; a provision is ambiguous when there is doubt or\nuncertainty about its meaning so that it is open to at least two\nreasonable interpretations; words get their plain and ordinary\nmeaning, viewed \"as the mass of mankind would view it\"; intent\nis gathered \"not from particular words and phrases, but from the\nentire agreement\"; extrinsic evidence \"may not be used to create\nan ambiguity within the contract where one may not exist\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How does an Arkansas court decide whether a\ncontract is ambiguous, and when may it look outside the\ncontract?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A four-corners reading of the entire document to decide\n    whether there is ambiguity\n    2. Words given their plain and ordinary meaning\n    3. The entire agreement read together, not isolated words\n    4. If an ambiguity exists but the contract itself resolves\n    it, the court applies the contract as a matter of law\n    5. Only if the contract's terms \"cannot sweep away the\n    initial uncertainty\" may the court look outside the\n    contract, and then \"[c]onsiderable weight\" may go to how the\n    parties understood the terms, as shown by their later\n    statements, acts, and conduct\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is why the Prochazka majority did not\nconsider the parties' affidavits until after it found an\nambiguity from the face of the contract\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: This sequence adds a middle step that the\ncasebook's basic textualist model does not show: an ambiguity\nfound at Stage One may still be resolved as a matter of law if\nother parts of the contract clear it up\n\n[0m[1mRule: Prochazka holding — a termination clause's placement and[0m\n[0m[1mstructural context can make it ambiguous[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding, Prochazka v. Bee-Three Development, LLC\n(Ark. Ct. App. 2015)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A buyer's right to terminate \"in its sole and\nabsolute discretion\" if the property is \"not suitable for\nBuyer's intended use\" is ambiguous on a four-corners reading\nwhen the clause sits inside an article about inspecting the\nproperty, the contract has other termination clauses tied to the\nsubject matter of their own sections, and \"intended use\" is\nundefined; it can reasonably be read either as absolute or as\nrequiring that termination be \"substantially related\" to the\npurposes of the inspection period\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can the placement of a termination clause\nwithin a particular article, together with the contract's\noverall structure, make a facially broad \"sole and absolute\ndiscretion\" clause ambiguous?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Read the disputed clause in light of the entire\n    agreement, including its location and headings\n    2. Compare it with parallel provisions (here, the\n    termination clauses in § 4.6 \"Utilities\" and § 5.2\n    \"Covenants\")\n    3. Ask whether the drafters could have written a standalone,\n    unconditional termination provision (as in the \"termination\n    for convenience\" clause in Harris Corp. v. Giesting &\n    Assoc., which the Eleventh Circuit held unambiguous)\n    4. Consider whether key terms (e.g., \"intended use\") are\n    undefined\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Use this when arguing that broad discretionary\nlanguage is limited by its structural context; a finding of\nambiguity defeats summary judgment and sends the meaning to a\ntrier of fact\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Four dissenting judges and the concurring judge\nread the language as unambiguous; the concurrence agreed with\nremand only because it saw a genuine fact issue about the\nbuyer's \"intended use\" and whether the property was suitable for\nit\n\n[0m[1mRule: Summary judgment standard (as applied in Prochazka)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case statement of law, Prochazka v. Bee-Three\nDevelopment, LLC, citing Benton Cnty. v. Overland Dev. Co. (Ark.\n2007)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Summary judgment is proper when there are no\ngenuine issues of material fact for a trier of fact and the\nmoving party is entitled to judgment as a matter of law; once\nthe movant makes a prima facie showing, the opponent must \"meet\nproof with proof,\" and on appeal the evidence is viewed in the\nlight most favorable to the non-movant, with all doubts and\ninferences resolved against the movant\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When may a court decide a contract dispute\nwithout a trial?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. No genuine issue of material fact\n    2. The movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law\n    3. Once the movant makes a prima facie case, the opponent\n    must answer with proof showing a material fact issue\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: An unambiguous contract allows summary judgment;\nan ambiguous one usually creates a fact question that defeats it\nunless the extrinsic evidence is one-sided\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Appellate review usually looks at affidavits and\nother documents, but in Prochazka the court set the affidavits\naside at the ambiguity stage because extrinsic evidence cannot\nbe used to create an ambiguity\n\n[0m[1mRule: Conflicting interpretations alone do not create ambiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Casebook Note 3, quoting Columbia Gas Transmission\nCorp. v. New Ulm Gas, Ltd. (Tex. 1996)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: \"An ambiguity does not arise simply because the\nparties advance conflicting interpretations of the contract\";\nunder textualism, the agreement is ambiguous only when its\nlanguage is in fact patently ambiguous\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Does the parties' disagreement about meaning,\nby itself, make a contract ambiguous?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The contract language itself must actually be\nambiguous on its face\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This blocks the argument that a dispute alone\nrequires a trial; otherwise every contract in litigation would\nbe \"ambiguous\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated\n\n[0m[1mRule: Types of patent ambiguity — vagueness and either/or[0m\n[0m[1mambiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Casebook Note 3, citing Farnsworth § 7.8 and Burton §\n1.2.2\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: There are two basic types of patent ambiguity: (1)\nvagueness, where the uncertainty is a matter of degree (e.g.,\n\"during the day,\" \"blue\"), and (2) either/or ambiguity, which\ncan arise in single words or short phrases (\"term ambiguity,\"\ne.g., \"bank\"), in sentences, or across the entire contract\n(\"structural ambiguity,\" the classic case being two conflicting\nprovisions)\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: In what forms can an ambiguity appear on the\nface of a contract?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Vagueness: a borderline zone where it is uncertain\n    whether performance complies (dusk or dawn under \"during the\n    day\")\n    2. Either/or: a choice between distinct meanings, arising at\n    the word or phrase, sentence, or whole-contract level\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: These categories help you spot ambiguities when\nreading contracts; the casebook asks which type was arguably\npresent in Prochazka (arguably structural, from the placement of\n§ 4.3 in Article 4 next to the other section-specific\ntermination clauses, and possibly term-based or vague, since\n\"intended use\" is undefined)\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Courts seldom use the distinction and usually call\nboth categories simply \"ambiguities\"\n\n[0m[1mRule: Ambiguity \"in the contested respect\"[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Casebook Note 4, citing Agrigenetics, Inc. v. Pioneer\nHi-Bred Int'l, Inc. (S.D. Ind. 2010), Burton pp. 105-106,\nHorseshoe Bay Resort, Ltd. v. CRVI CDP Portfolio, LLC (Tex. App.\n2013), and Gastar Exploration, Inc. v. Rine (W. Va. 2017)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A contract is not patently ambiguous merely because\nit is reasonably susceptible to more than one meaning; it is\npatently ambiguous only if it is reasonably susceptible to both\nof the meanings the litigants argue for (\"Ambiguity exists only\nwhen both parties' interpretive positions [are] reasonable\"); a\ncourt may find ambiguity even when both parties say the contract\nis unambiguous\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Whose interpretations must be reasonable for a\ncontract to be ambiguous, and what rulings can a court make at\nStage One?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Identify each litigant's proposed meaning\n    2. Ask whether the language is reasonably susceptible to\n    both proposed meanings\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: At Stage One the court can rule that (1) the\ncontract unambiguously means what the plaintiff claims, (2) it\nunambiguously means what the defendant claims, (3) it\nunambiguously means something in between, or (4) it is ambiguous\nand goes to Stage Two; in Gastar, the grantee argued the deed\nunambiguously transferred all oil and gas rights and the\ngrantors argued it transferred none, and the West Virginia\nSupreme Court found both readings plausible and also found that\nthe deed may have transferred half, so with three plausible\nreadings it held the deed ambiguous (option 4)\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Had the deed's language been different, the court\ncould have chosen option 1, 2, or 3\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: This qualification answers Holmes's critique that\nwords neve",
        "r have just one meaning (Note 7); textualists need not\nclaim words have a single meaning, only that the text is not\nreasonably susceptible to both contested meanings\n\n[0m[1mRule: The text of the contract is the most important[0m\n[0m[1minterpretive evidence[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Casebook Note 5, citing AmeriPath, Inc. v. Hebert\n(Tex. App. 2014), Blind Hunting Club, LLC v. Martini (Ind. Ct.\nApp. 2021), and Ray v. William G. Eurice Bros., Inc. (Md. 1952)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Under both textualism and contextualism, the\nwritten words are the most important evidence of meaning; the\n\"text of the contract\" includes operative clauses, titles and\nheadings, recitals, and all writings that make up the agreement,\nincluding exhibits, appendices, and documents incorporated by\nreference\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What counts as the \"text\" a court reads when\ninterpreting a contract?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Operative clauses: the provisions stating the parties'\n    obligations\n    2. Titles and headings: these organize clauses or name the\n    whole contract\n    3. Recitals: \"whereas\" clauses explaining why the parties\n    contracted or the background facts\n    4. Related documents: exhibits, appendices, and writings\n    incorporated by reference (e.g., the plans and\n    specifications in Ray)\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Lawyers argue from headings and structure (as the\nProchazka majority did with Article 4 \"Inspection of Property\")\nand from recitals, where drafters often try to state their\nclients' contractual intent\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated\n\n[0m[1mRule: Dictionaries are not extrinsic evidence[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Casebook narrative and Note 6, citing Burton §§ 2.1.2,\n4.2.3, Fireman's Fund Ins. Co. v. Great Am. Ins. Co. (S.D.N.Y.\n2014), and Anchor Savs. Bank, FSB v. United States (Fed. Cl.\n2015)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Courts frequently consult dictionaries when\ninterpreting contracts, statutes, and constitutions;\ndictionaries generally are not \"evidence\" under the rules of\nevidence and usually are not treated as extrinsic evidence, so\ntextualist judges may use them at Stage One\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: May a textualist court use a dictionary in\ndeciding whether a contract is ambiguous?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: None beyond the court's discretion to consult\nthe source\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Dictionaries are the one outside source a\nfour-corners court can use to establish ordinary meaning\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading says courts \"usually,\" not always,\ndecline to treat dictionaries as extrinsic evidence\n\n[0m[1mRule: Holmes/Corbin critique of textualism[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Casebook Note 7, quoting Holmes, The Theory of Legal\nInterpretation (1899), and citing 5 Corbin on Contracts § 24.7\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Critics reject textualism because a word or\ncollocation of words does not have \"one meaning and no other,\"\nand meaning can never be plain without considering extrinsic\nevidence of \"at least some of the surrounding circumstances\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can contract language ever be \"plain\" without\nlooking at context?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Not applicable; this is a theoretical critique\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Use it in policy questions comparing textualism\nand contextualism; the casebook asks whether the critique fairly\ndescribes what textualists mean by \"unambiguous,\" given the\ncontested-respect doctrine\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The contested-respect rule (Note 4) arguably\nanswers the critique, since textualists need not claim a single\nmeaning, only that the text cannot reasonably bear both\ncontested meanings\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (First) of Contracts § 227 — Standard of[0m\n[0m[1minterpretation (objective vs. subjective)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative citing Restatement (First) § 227 &\ncmt a, 11 Williston § 31:1, Behrens v. S.P. Constr. Co. (N.H.\n2006), Restatement (Second) § 202 cmt b, and Taylor v.\nNationsBank, N.A. (Md. 2001)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A standard of interpretation is \"the test applied\nby the law to words and to other manifestations of intention in\norder to determine the meaning to be given to them\"; objective\nstandards ask what a reasonable person would understand the\ncontract to mean, while subjective standards ask what one or\nboth parties actually understood it to mean; both are applied as\nof the time the contract was formed\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Once a contract is found ambiguous, what test\ndetermines its meaning?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Objective: put a reasonable person in the parties'\n    position and ask what that person would expect the term to\n    mean under the circumstances\n    2. Subjective: ask what the parties actually understood\n    3. Either way, measure meaning at the time of formation\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is applied at Stage Two to resolve a patent\n(or latent) ambiguity\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (First) of Contracts § 230 — The leading[0m\n[0m[1mobjective standard (Williston)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative citing Restatement (First) § 230,\ncmt b, and Illustration 1\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: The contract means what a reasonable person would\nunderstand it to mean if that person knew all the surrounding\ncircumstances, including the contract, standard English,\nindustry practice, the preliminary negotiations, and other\ncontext; Williston defended this standard even when it produces\na meaning neither party intended\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Under the objective approach, whose\nunderstanding controls an ambiguous term?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A hypothetical reasonable person\n    2. Who is familiar with all the surrounding circumstances\n    3. Measured at the time of formation\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Illustration 1: A means to sell only English\npatents and B understands the deal to cover English, French, and\nAmerican patents; if a reasonably intelligent person would read\nthe agreement as English and American but not French patents,\nthere is a contract on that meaning, which neither party held\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None in the Restatement (First) formulation; it is\ncriticized by Corbin and rejected by the Restatement (Second)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 201(1) — Shared[0m\n[0m[1mmeaning governs (Step 1)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Restatement (Second) § 201(1), as described in the\ntextbook narrative; derived from Corbin's two questions (3\nCorbin § 536): whose meaning controls, and what was that meaning\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: If both parties attach the same meaning to contract\nlanguage, that meaning governs, even if it differs from what a\nreasonable person would understand\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Under the Restatement (Second)'s subjective\nstandard, what happens when the parties actually shared a\nmeaning?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Both parties actually attached the same meaning\nto the language at formation\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is the first step in resolving an ambiguity;\nthe court considers the same materials as under the objective\nstandard (language, dictionaries, and all or virtually all\nextrinsic evidence); if the parties did not share a meaning, go\nto § 201(2)\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None; reflects Corbin's view that imposing a\nmeaning neither party held would \"hold justice up to ridicule\"\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Unlike Restatement (First) § 230, a court will\nnever impose a third party's reasonable meaning that differs\nfrom what the parties understood or had reason to understand (5\nCorbin § 24.6)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 201(2)(a) — Actual[0m\n[0m[1mknowledge (Step 2)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Restatement (Second) § 201(2)(a) & cmt b, as explained\nin the textbook narrative\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: If one party actually knew what the other party\ntook the contract to mean, but the other party had only\nconstructive knowledge or no knowledge of the first party's\nmeaning, the second party's interpretation governs\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When the parties attached different meanings,\nwhose meaning controls if one party actually knew the other's\nmeaning?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The parties attached different meanings\n    2. Party A actually knew Party B's meaning\n    3. Party B had only constructive knowledge or no knowledge\n    of Party A's meaning\n    4. Result: Party B's meaning governs\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The relevant knowledge is each party's knowledge\nof the other party's understanding, not of the \"true\" meaning;\ncommentators call this a \"fault\" standard in which the\nless-at-fault (less-knowing) party wins (Burton § 2.5,\nFarnsworth § 7.9)\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: If both parties had the same level of knowledge,\ngo to § 201(3)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 201(2)(b) —[0m\n[0m[1mConstructive knowledge (Step 2)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Restatement (Second) § 201(2)(b), as explained in the\ntextbook narrative\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: If one party should have known what the other party\ntook the contract to mean, but the other party had no knowledge\nof the first party's meaning, the second party's interpretation\ngoverns\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When the parties attached different meanings,\nwhose meaning controls if one party had reason to know the\nother's meaning?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The parties attached different meanings\n    2. Party A had constructive knowledge (should have known) of\n    Party B's meaning\n    3. Party B had no knowledge of Party A's meaning\n    4. Result: Party B's meaning governs\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: There are three levels of knowledge, ranked\nactual > constructive > none; the party with less knowledge of\nthe other's meaning wins\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Equal levels of knowledge trigger § 201(3)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 201(3) — No meeting of[0m\n[0m[1mthe minds; treat as a gap (Step 3)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Restatement (Second) § 201(3), § 201 cmt d, § 204, §\n212 Illustration 1, as described in the textbook narrative\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: If the parties had the same level of knowledge of\neach other's meaning (both actual, both constructive, or both\nnone), neither is bound by the other's meaning; there is \"no\nmeeting of the minds,\" and courts generally treat the disputed\nterm as an indefinite term, effectively a gap\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What happens when neither party's meaning\nprevails under § 201?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Equal levels of knowledge\n    2. If the gap concerns a non-material term, the court fills\n    it with a term reasonable in the circumstances (§ 201 cmt d,\n    § 204)\n    3. If the gap concerns a material term and the parties have\n    not performed, or have performed so little that performance\n    can be undone, there is no contract (§ 212 Illus. 1: A means\n    Blackacre, B means Whiteacre, the writing reasonably\n    describes Greenacre, neither has more reason to know of the\n    misdescription, so there is no contract)\n    4. If the gap concerns a material term and performance has\n    gone too far to undo, the court fills it with a reasonable\n    term (§ 204)\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This links interpretation back to the\nindefiniteness doctrine from Chapter 2, Section A.4; gap-filling\nis covered further in Chapter 6\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Professor Prince argues that in these gap\nsituations courts should not strain to apply interpretive\nprinciples but should weigh equitable factors to reach a \"just\nresult\": public interests and the public good, sharing\nunanticipated losses and gains, avoiding windfalls, and the\nparties' good or bad faith (31 Loy. L.A. L. Rev. 557); this is a\nscholarly proposal, not a stated rule\n\n[0m[1mRule: Parol evidence rule (preview only)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative introducing Chapter 5, Section B\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: When parties have put their agreement in writing\nand one party later claims the writing does not accurately or\ncompletely state their \"true\" agreement (because of additional\nagreements not in the writing, or because the writing is\ninaccurate or misleading), courts screen evidence of those\nmatters through the \"parol evidence rule\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When may a party introduce evidence of\nagreements outside a written contract?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The rule arises only when the parties have\nchosen to put their agreement in writing\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The attached pages only introduce the rule and\nsay it is closely related to interpretation; its meaning and\napplication are covered in Section B, which is not in this\nreading\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The attached reading does not state the rule's\nelements or exceptions; despite the class title, pp. 387-406 are\nalmost entirely about interpretation and the ambiguity\ndetermination\n[0m[1m2. Case Narrative[0m\n[0m[1mProchazka v. Bee-Three Development, LLC[0m\nRobert and Donna Prochazka were individuals who owned a\ncommercial lot. Bee-Three Development, LLC was a development\ncompany; one of its managing members was Rob Holliday.\nBee-Three's business model, according to Holliday, was to put a\nproperty under contract, make sure it was \"viable\" for the\n\"specifications of the end user,\" improve it, and lease it to a\nclient (the end user). The caption names the Prochazkas first\nbecause they were the appealing parties. In the lawsuit itself,\nBee-Three was the plaintiff and the Prochazkas were the\ndefendants (and counterclaimants). The opinion does not give\ndates for the underlying events; it was decided in 2015. It does\nnot say exactly where the lot was, though the lawsuit was filed\nin Pope County, Arkansas.\nBee-Three signed a written purchase agreement to buy the\nProchazkas' commercial lot and paid \$7,000 in earnest money.\nArticle 4 of the agreement, titled \"Inspection of Property,\"\ncreated an inspection period. During that period, § 4.1 let the\nbuyer run soil, engineering, environmental, and geotechnical\ntests; check permits, licenses, and government requirements for\nits intended use; confirm the lot could be platted as a separate\ntax lot and had adequate utilities and access; and \"determine\ngenerally the desirability and utility of the Property for\nBuyer's planned use.\" After the inspection period expired, the\nearnest money became non-refundable and \"at-risk.\" Section 4.3,\n\"Right to Terminate,\" said that if the buyer determined \"in its\nsole and absolute discretion, that the Property is not suitable\nfor Buyer's intended use,\" it could terminate by written notice\non or before the last day of the inspection period and get its\nearnest money back (less the independent consideration). The\ncontract also had two other termination clauses: § 4.6\n(\"Utilities\"), allowing termination if certain utility-related\nstatements proved untrue, and § 5.2 (\"Covenants\"), allowing\nremedies including termination for untrue or misleading seller\nrepresentations. The contract did not define \"intended use.\"\nBee-Three kept its real purpose secret. It had an end-user\nclient lined up as a prospective tenant, but according to Robert\nProchazka it never disclosed that client, and there were no\ndiscussions about the deal depending on Bee-Three finding a\ntenant. During the inspection period, that client \"changed their\nmind\" and renewed its lease at its current location. As Holliday\nput it, \"without a tenant or user we have no need to purchase\nthe land and develop the property.\" Bee-Three sent notice\nterminating the agreement within the inspection period and\ndemanded its \$7,000 back. Bee-Three did not tie the termination\nto any physical, engineering, permitting, utility, or tax\nproblem found during inspection. The Prochazkas believed\ntermination under § 4.3 had to relate to the inspection-period\nconcerns, and they refused to return the money.\nBee-Three sued the Prochazkas to recover the \$7,000 earnest\nmoney. The Prochazkas counterclaimed for breach of contract and\nasked that the earnest money be forfeited to them as liquidated\ndamages.\n[0m[1mOther cases mentioned in the reading[0m\nThe reading mentions several other cases only briefly, to\nillustrate a point, and does not reproduce their opinions.\nExamples include Gastar Exploration, Inc. v. Rine (deed and oil\nand gas rights), Harris Corp. v. Giesting & Assoc.\n(termination-for-convenience clause), and Ray v. William G.\nEurice Bros. (incorporation by reference). The attached pages\ngive no underlying facts beyond those one-line descriptions, so\nthey are not narrated or briefed here. The reading ends by\nintroducing Frigaliment and listing study questions, but the\nFrigaliment opinion is not in pp. 387-406, so it cannot be\nsummarized from the attached material.\n[0m[1m3. Cases[0m\n[0m[1mCase Name: Prochazka v. Bee-Three Development, LLC[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 387-406\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Contracts\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - Bee-Three agreed in writing to buy a commercial lot from\n    Robert and Donna Prochazka and paid \$7,000 in earnest money\n    - Article 4 (\"Inspection of Property\") set an inspection\n    period; § 4.1 listed inspection purposes (soil, engineering,\n    environmental tests; permits and government requirements;\n    platting as a separate tax lot; utilities; access) and also\n    let the buyer \"determine generally the desirability and\n    utility of the Property for Buyer's planned use\"\n    - Section 4.3 let the buyer terminate by written notice\n    within the inspection period if it determined \"in its sole\n    and absolute discretion, that the Property is not suitable\n    for Buyer's intended use,\" with the earnest money returned;\n    after the inspection period, the earnest money became\n    non-refundable and \"at-risk\"\n    - The contract had two other termination clauses, each\n    placed in a subject-specific section: § 4.6 (\"Utilities\")\n    and § 5.2 (\"Covenants\")\n    - \"Intended use\" was not defined anywhere in the contract\n    - Bee-Three terminated during the inspection period because\n    its undisclosed prospective tenant (end user) renewed its\n    existing lease elsewhere; Bee-Three did not link the\n    termination to any physical, engineering, permitting, or tax\n    problem\n    - Robert Prochazka's affidavit said there were no\n    discussions about the contract depending on Bee-Three\n    securing a tenant and that Bee-Three kept its purpose and\n    end-user client secret\n    - Rob Holliday's affidavit for Bee-Three said Bee-Three's\n    \"end use\" was to improve and lease the property to a client,\n    and without that tenant it had no need to buy\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Pope County Circuit Court (Arkansas trial court): granted\n    Bee-Three's (Plaintiff/Counter-Defendant) motion for summary\n    judgment, holding that § 4.3 unambiguously gave Bee-Three an\n    \"absolute\" right to terminate during the inspection period;\n    awarded Bee-Three the \$7,000 earnest money and costs and\n    disposed of the Prochazkas' (Defendants/Counterclaimants)\n    counterclaim\n    - Appeal (the opinion states only that the Prochazkas\n    \"appeal that decision\"): the Prochazkas\n    (Defendants/Counterclaimants-Appellants) sought review in\n    the Arkansas Court of Appeals on the grounds that the\n    termination language in § 4.3 is ambiguous because it\n    reasonably has more than one meaning, and the circuit court\n    erred \"when it substituted its opinion as the meaning of the\n    language\" instead of leaving it \"to the trier-of-fact to\n    determine the intent of the parties\"\n    - Arkansas Court of Appeals: t",
        "he Prochazkas\n    (Defendants/Counterclaimants below) are the appellants;\n    Bee-Three Development, LLC (Plaintiff below) is the appellee\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether, on a four-corners reading of the entire\npurchase agreement, § 4.3's grant of a right to terminate \"in\n[the buyer's] sole and absolute discretion\" if the property is\nunsuitable for the buyer's \"intended use\" is reasonably open to\nmore than one interpretation (an absolute right versus a right\ntied to the purposes of the inspection period), so that summary\njudgment for Bee-Three was improper?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The Arkansas Court of Appeals held that § 4.3 is\nambiguous when the entire contract is considered, because it can\nreasonably be read either as an absolute termination right or as\nrequiring that termination be \"substantially related\" to the\npurpose of the inspection period and the concerns in Article 4;\nthe ambiguity cannot be resolved from the contract itself, and\nthe affidavits show a genuine issue of material fact, so summary\njudgment was improper\n- [0m[1mRule[0m: Prochazka holding — a termination clause's placement and\nstructural context can make it ambiguous: a \"sole and absolute\ndiscretion\" termination right placed inside an inspection\narticle, among other subject-specific termination clauses and\nwith \"intended use\" undefined, is reasonably open to two\ninterpretations, and its meaning must go to a trier of fact (see\nalso Arkansas interpretation principles applied in Prochazka;\nSummary judgment standard; Textualism Stage One and Stage Two)\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The Arkansas Court of Appeals reasoned in these\nsteps:\n    - It first asked whether § 4.3 is ambiguous on a\n    four-corners reading and deliberately did not consider the\n    affidavits at this stage, because extrinsic evidence \"may\n    not be used to create an ambiguity within the contract where\n    one may not exist\"\n    - It noted that the contract's three termination clauses\n    each sit in different, subject-specific sections (§ 4.3\n    Inspection, § 4.6 Utilities, § 5.2 Covenants), which\n    supports reading each clause as \"anchored more or less to\n    the subject matter of the sections in which they appear\"\n    - It acknowledged that § 4.3 could reasonably be read as\n    \"absolute,\" as the circuit court and the dissent read it,\n    but held it could also reasonably be read as requiring\n    termination to be \"substantially related\" to the inspection\n    period's purposes\n    - It asked, \"Why else place a termination clause under\n    Article 4?\" and \"Why not make the argued for 'absolute right\n    to terminate' provision a standalone provision?\",\n    contrasting the unambiguous termination-for-convenience\n    clause in Harris Corp. v. Giesting\n    - It pointed out that \"intended use\" is not defined in the\n    contract\n    - Having found an ambiguity the contract itself could not\n    resolve, it then turned to the affidavits and found a\n    genuine fact dispute: Holliday's affidavit did not connect\n    the lost tenant to anything discovered during inspection,\n    while Prochazka's affidavit denied any tenant contingency\n    and described Bee-Three's secrecy\n    - How the parties intended the ambiguous agreement to\n    operate is a question for the trier of fact\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The opinion does not set out\nBee-Three's appellate brief separately; its position appears\nthrough the circuit court ruling it obtained and its affidavit:\n    - The words \"sole and absolute discretion\" make the\n    termination right absolute \"by the very words that created\n    it\"\n    - Linking the \"right to terminate\" paragraph to the \"right\n    to inspect\" paragraph has no support in the contract and\n    imports an ambiguity \"where there really is none\"\n    - Through Holliday's affidavit: its \"end use\" was to improve\n    and lease the property to an end-user client; once the\n    client renewed its lease elsewhere, the property was no\n    longer suitable for its intended use, and it terminated\n    within the inspection period as § 4.3 allows\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: The Prochazkas argued:\n    - § 4.3 reasonably has more than one meaning; it can be read\n    as the circuit court read it, or as requiring termination to\n    relate to an intended purpose of the inspection period under\n    § 4.1 (permits, engineering tests, utilities, access, tax\n    issues, and the like)\n    - Given reasonable differences of opinion, the circuit court\n    wrongly substituted its own view of the meaning instead of\n    leaving the parties' intent to the trier of fact\n    - Factually (Prochazka affidavit): there was never any\n    discussion of a tenant contingency, and Bee-Three hid its\n    true purpose and its end-user client, so it should not be\n    allowed to terminate under these circumstances\n    - On the counterclaim: Bee-Three's termination was a breach,\n    entitling the Prochazkas to keep the earnest money as\n    liquidated damages\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The Arkansas Court of Appeals reversed the\nsummary judgment, reinstated the Prochazkas' counterclaim, and\nremanded for further proceedings (Harrison, J., joined by\nAbramson, Kinard, and Whiteaker, JJ.; Virden, J., concurring;\nGruber, J., dissenting, joined by Vaught, Hoofman, and Brown,\nJJ.); after remand the parties settled by splitting the \$7,000\nevenly\n    - Concurrence (Virden, J.): saw no ambiguity in the\n    termination language but agreed with remand because there\n    was a genuine issue of material fact about the buyer's\n    \"intended use\" and whether the sellers' property was\n    suitable for it\n    - Dissent (Gruber, J.): would have affirmed; the language is\n    clear; § 4.1 itself let Bee-Three \"determine generally\" the\n    property's desirability for its planned use, § 4.3 gave\n    \"sole and absolute\" discretion to find the property\n    unsuitable for its intended use (here, leasing to an anchor\n    tenant), Bee-Three did not have to disclose that use, and\n    finding an ambiguity improperly \"enlarge[s] and extend[s]\n    the terms of the contract\"\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m: This is the casebook's model of how a textualist\ncourt decides whether a contract is patently ambiguous, and you\nshould take several lessons from it:\n    - It shows the four-corners method at work, with the court\n    refusing to look at the affidavits until after it found an\n    ambiguity in the text (this answers Note 1's question about\n    why the case was \"a bit different\")\n    - It shows that structure, placement, headings, and parallel\n    provisions are textual evidence that can create a structural\n    ambiguity even around broad language like \"sole and absolute\n    discretion\"\n    - The 5-4 split, with a concurrence that found no ambiguity,\n    shows how contestable the ambiguity call is and that this\n    call often decides whether the case ends on summary judgment\n    - It shows the procedural stakes of the ambiguity finding:\n    an ambiguity (with conflicting extrinsic evidence) sends the\n    case to a trier of fact\n    - It is a vehicle for the classification questions in Note 3\n    (vagueness vs. either/or, including structural and term\n    ambiguity) and for the policy point in Note 2 about why\n    parties litigate over a modest \$7,000\n[0m[1m4. Definitions[0m\n[0m[1mContract interpretation[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The process of determining the meaning of\ncontractual language, aimed at ascertaining the parties' intent\n(R2d § 200)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the subject lawyers working with contracts\ndeal with most often, both when drafting to avoid disputes and\nwhen litigating disputes after formation\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Some authorities separate it from \"construction,\"\nbut the casebook folds construction into interpretation\n\n[0m[1mConstruction[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The court's role in determining the legal effect\nof contractual language\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The casebook generally does not use it as a\nseparate concept\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Interpretation, in the narrow sense, is about the\nmeaning the parties attached to the words; construction is about\ntheir legal effect\n\n[0m[1mTextualism (formalist / classical approach)[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An interpretive approach in which the Stage One\nambiguity determination is made from the four corners of the\ncontract alone, without extrinsic evidence\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Most scholars believe it is the majority\napproach; Prochazka applies it\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It differs from contextualism only at Stage One;\nboth allow extrinsic evidence at Stage Two\n\n[0m[1mContextualism (substantive / modern approach)[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An interpretive approach in which the judge\nconsiders both the text and extrinsic evidence in deciding at\nStage One whether a contract is ambiguous\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The Restatement (Second) endorses it; the\ncasebook covers it in more depth in Section B\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It recognizes latent as well as patent\nambiguities; textualism recognizes only patent ambiguities\n\n[0m[1mPlain meaning rule / four corners rule[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The textualist two-stage rule: decide ambiguity\nfrom the face of the contract, apply the plain meaning if the\ncontract is unambiguous, and resolve the ambiguity with\nextrinsic evidence only if it is ambiguous\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the doctrinal core of textualism and\nallows early disposition of a case\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: \"Plain meaning\" is the result when no ambiguity\nis found; \"four corners\" describes what the court may look at in\nmaking that call\n\n[0m[1mAmbiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Contract language that is \"reasonably susceptible\"\nto more than one meaning; in Arkansas's phrasing, open to at\nleast two reasonable interpretations\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The threshold question at Stage One, which\ndecides whether extrinsic evidence comes in (under textualism)\nand whether the case goes to a factfinder\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The parties' disagreement alone does not create\nambiguity (Columbia Gas); the language must be reasonably\nsusceptible to both contested meanings\n\n[0m[1mPatent (intrinsic / facial) ambiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An ambiguity that appears on the face of the\ncontract\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the only kind textualism recognizes as a\ngateway to extrinsic evidence\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Compare latent ambiguity, which only context\nreveals\n\n[0m[1mLatent (extrinsic) ambiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An ambiguity established through extrinsic\nevidence of context, not visible from the text alone\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Contextualism recognizes it; textualist courts\nsuch as the Prochazka majority refuse to let extrinsic evidence\n\"create\" one\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Compare patent ambiguity\n\n[0m[1mVagueness[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A type of patent ambiguity involving a question of\ndegree, where there is a borderline zone of uncertain compliance\n(e.g., \"during the day\" at dusk or dawn; where \"blue\" shades\ninto green or purple)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is one of the two basic types of patent\nambiguity\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Vagueness is a spectrum problem, while either/or\nambiguity is a choice between distinct meanings; courts rarely\nuse this distinction\n\n[0m[1mEither/or ambiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A patent ambiguity where language could carry one\nof two or more distinct meanings, arising in words or phrases,\nsentences, or entire contracts\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the second basic type of patent ambiguity\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It includes term ambiguity (words or short\nphrases) and structural ambiguity (whole contracts); compare\nvagueness\n\n[0m[1mTerm ambiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An either/or ambiguity in an individual word or\nshort phrase, e.g., \"bank\" as a financial institution or a\nriverbank\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Familiar because many English words have several\nmeanings; \"intended use\" in Prochazka is arguably a candidate\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is confined to a word or phrase, unlike\nstructural ambiguity\n\n[0m[1mStructural ambiguity[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An either/or ambiguity arising from the contract\nas a whole, classically where two provisions conflict\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Arguably the kind found in Prochazka, from the\nplacement of § 4.3 within Article 4 and next to the\nsection-specific termination clauses\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It comes from how provisions relate to one\nanother, not from any single word's multiple meanings\n\n[0m[1mAmbiguity \"in the contested respect\"[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The requirement that the contract be reasonably\nsusceptible to both meanings the litigants argue for, not merely\nto more than one meaning in the abstract\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It narrows the ambiguity inquiry, answers\nHolmes's critique, and frames the four possible Stage One\nrulings (illustrated by Gastar)\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: A word can have several dictionary meanings and\nstill be unambiguous in the contested respect\n\n[0m[1mExtrinsic evidence[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: All evidence other than the words of the contract\nand dictionaries; literally, evidence external to the contract\n(e.g., documents and testimony about preliminary negotiations,\ntestimony about industry practice, affidavits)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Whether it may be used at Stage One is the\ndividing line between textualism and contextualism\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Dictionaries are usually not treated as extrinsic\nevidence\n\n[0m[1mOperative clauses (operative provisions)[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The words, phrases, sentences, and paragraphs that\nspecify the parties' obligations under the agreement\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Always present in written contracts and the core\ntext interpreted\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Compare headings, which organize, and recitals,\nwhich explain background\n\n[0m[1mTitles / headings[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Labels that classify or organize operative clauses\nor name the whole contract (e.g., \"Sales Agreement,\" \"Insurance\nPolicy\")\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: They are part of the text; in Prochazka, the\nArticle 4 heading \"Inspection of Property\" supported the\nmajority's finding of ambiguity\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: They are organizational, not obligation-creating\n\n[0m[1mRecitals[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Preliminary statements, usually beginning\n\"whereas,\" explaining why the parties entered the agreement or\nthe background facts\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Drafters often try to state their clients'\ncontractual intent in them, which makes them useful interpretive\nevidence\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Distinguished from operative provisions (Blind\nHunting Club)\n\n[0m[1mIncorporation by reference[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Expressly making another writing part of the\ncontract (e.g., the architectural plans and specifications in\nRay v. William G. Eurice Bros.)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The \"text of the contract\" includes every\nwriting that makes up the agreement, including exhibits and\nappendices\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Incorporated writings count as text, not\nextrinsic evidence\n\n[0m[1mStandard of interpretation[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: \"[T]he test applied by the law to words and to\nother manifestations of intention in order to determine the\nmeaning to be given to them\" (Restatement (First) § 227)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the tool used at Stage Two to resolve an\nambiguity\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: There are two basic types, objective and\nsubjective\n\n[0m[1mObjective standard[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Meaning is what a reasonable person in the\nparties' position, familiar with all the surrounding\ncircumstances, would understand at formation (Restatement\n(First) § 230; Behrens)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Williston defended it even when it yields a\nmeaning neither party intended (Illustration 1, the patents\nexample)\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It focuses on the reasonable person, not on what\nthe parties actually thought\n\n[0m[1mSubjective standard[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Meaning is what one or both parties actually\nunderstood at formation; the leading version is Restatement\n(Second) § 201, built on Corbin's work\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It never imposes a third party's meaning that\ndiffers from what the parties understood or had reason to\nunderstand\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It considers the same materials as the objective\nstandard but asks about the parties' actual and constructive\nknowledge rather than a reasonable outsider's view\n\n[0m[1mConstructive knowledge[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: What a party should have known about the other\nparty's understanding of the contract\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the middle of the three knowledge levels\n(actual, constructive, none) under § 201(2)\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Actual knowledge is what the party in fact knew;\nunder § 201 the relevant knowledge is always about the other\nparty's meaning\n\n[0m[1m\"Fault\" standard[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Commentators' description of § 201(2): the party\nless at fault, meaning the one with less knowledge of the\nother's meaning, wins the interpretive dispute\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: A convenient shorthand for applying §\n201(2)(a)-(b)\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: If fault is equal, § 201(3) applies and neither\nparty's meaning governs\n\n[0m[1mNo meeting of the minds[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The § 201(3) result when neither party is bound by\nthe other's meaning because their knowledge levels are equal\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The disputed term is treated as an indefinite\nterm or gap, which leads to gap-filling or no contract depending\non materiality and how far performance has gone\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It ties interpretation to the Chapter 2\nindefiniteness doctrine and the Chapter 6 gap-filling material\n\n[0m[1mGap[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A matter the contract does not effectively\naddress, here because an ambiguous term is treated as if it were\nmissing\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Non-material gaps, and material gaps where\nperformance cannot be undone, are filled with a reasonable term\n(§ 204); material gaps before substantial performance mean there\nis no contract\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Prince argues courts should fill such gaps by\nweighing equitable factors toward a \"just result\" rather than\nstraining to interpret\n\n[0m[1mParol evidence rule[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The rule courts use to screen evidence that a\nwritten agreement does not accurately or completely state the\nparties' \"true\" agreement, such as additional agreements not in\nthe writing\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is closely related to interpretation and\narises only for written agreements; Section B, not these pages,\nexplains it\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Interpretation asks what the written terms mean;\nthe parol evidence rule asks whether outside agreements may\nsupplement or contradict the writing\n\n[0m[1mSummary judgment[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Judgment without trial when there is no genuine\nissue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as\na matter of law\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the usual vehicle for deciding an\nunambiguous contract, or an ambiguous one with one-sided\nextrinsic evidence; it was reversed in Prochazka\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The opponent must \"meet proof with proof\"; all\ndoubts are resolved against the movant\n\n[0m[1mEarnest money / liquidated damages[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Earnest money is a buyer's deposit toward a\npurchase; liquidated damages are a pre-set damages amount, which\nthe Prochazkas sought by asking that the earnest money be\nforfeited to them\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The \$7,000 in earnest money was the entire\namount at stake in Prochazka, and the case settled with the\nparties splitting it evenly\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: U",
        "nder § 4.1 the earnest money became\nnon-refundable and \"at-risk\" only after the inspection period\nexpired\n\n[0m[1mTermination for convenience[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A clause letting a party end the contract for any\nreason, e.g., \"may terminate this Agreement for convenience at\nany time upon sixty days written notice\" (Harris Corp. v.\nGiesting)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The Prochazka majority used it as the contrast\ncase: a standalone, unconditional clause is unambiguous, whereas\n§ 4.3 was embedded in the inspection article\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: A standalone convenience clause is not tied to\nany subject matter; a clause placed within a subject-specific\narticle may be read as limited by that subject\n\nDespite the class title, pp. 387-406 only introduce the parol\nevidence rule; the rule itself is covered in Section B, and the\nFrigaliment opinion that the reading sets up is not in these\npages. Separately, the Google Drive connector needs to be\nauthorized in your claude.ai connector settings before it can be\nused."
    )
    val codePoints: IntArray
        get() = textChunks.joinToString("").codePoints().toArray()
}

interface GlyphAssemblyStrategy {
    fun assemble(codePoints: IntArray): String
}

class SequentialGlyphAssemblyStrategy : GlyphAssemblyStrategy {
    override fun assemble(codePoints: IntArray): String {
        val builder = StringBuilder()
        for (codePoint in codePoints) {
            builder.appendCodePoint(codePoint)
        }
        return builder.toString()
    }
}

class DocumentAssemblyEngine(private val strategy: GlyphAssemblyStrategy) {
    fun render(): String = strategy.assemble(CodePointRepository.codePoints)
}

class ConsoleOutputSink {
    fun writeLine(payload: String) = println(payload)
}

class DocumentCompilerFacade {
    private val engine = DocumentAssemblyEngine(SequentialGlyphAssemblyStrategy())
    private val sink = ConsoleOutputSink()

    fun execute() {
        sink.writeLine(engine.render())
    }
}

fun main() {
    DocumentCompilerFacade().execute()
}
